      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x}$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-2x+2-(-{x}^{2}+2x+1)){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}{({x}_{n}^{2}-4{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(-{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}}{({x}_{0}^{2}-4{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(-(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}}{((1.5)^{2}-4(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}} \\\\  & ≈2.136,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.",
